Concurrency Control Manager (CCM)

Class diagram: docs/ccm-class-diagram.drawio (PNG export next to it).
Contract: IConcurrencyControlManager and IFailureRecoveryManager follow the SQLike supergroup report (section 2.1).

Current status (Milestone 2)
- Every IConcurrencyControlManager method, including the PRD section 3.4 additions (requestWriteAccess,
  requestSchemaWrite, beginApply/endApply, awaitNoActiveTransactions(timeoutMs)), is implemented by
  sqlike.ccm.ConcurrencyControlManager.
- Locking is table level (PRD section 9). RECORD requests lock their table. The catalog is its own resource,
  the reserved table name __catalog__ (a scope named CATALOG is recognized too, see sqlike.ccm.Resources).
  Default protocol: LOCK_BASED (rigorous 2PL, FIFO queue, S to X upgrade). The active protocol is logged and
  printed when CCM starts.
- Deadlocks: wait-for graph, checked every 100 ms, victim is the youngest transaction. A two-transaction
  deadlock is resolved in under 5 seconds (tested). The victim goes through the normal abort flow.
- TIMESTAMP_BASED is strict timestamp ordering (timestamp = txId) on table-level resources.
- Lifecycle: ACTIVE, PARTIALLY_COMMITTED, FAILED, ABORTED, COMMITTED with validated transitions. New
  transactions are refused until initAfterRecovery has run. pauseNewTransactions holds only new
  transactions; running ones can still commit or abort. initAfterRecovery does not reopen a paused gate.
- Write flow: requestWriteAccess (lock only), then requestWrite (one change record, changeLSN), then
  beginApply/endApply around the SM mutation. Abort and commit wait for a running apply (30 s by default).
- Commit: PARTIALLY_COMMITTED, logCommit, COMMITTED, release locks. Abort: FAILED, rollback, logAbort,
  ABORTED, release locks. rollback and logAbort are tried 3 times, 100 ms apart (CCMConfig). If they still
  fail, the transaction stays FAILED with its locks held and abort can be called again.
- FRM failures are RuntimeExceptions. CCM reports them to QP as WAL_FAILURE.
- Deviation from the course spec (CC-03): if logCommit fails, CCM does not abort. COMMIT may already be
  durable, so the transaction stays PARTIALLY_COMMITTED, its locks stay held, and CCM refuses new
  transactions until initAfterRecovery (PRD section 11).

Packages (src/main/java)
- sqlike.contract  IConcurrencyControlManager (CCM) and IFailureRecoveryManager (FRM), copied from the contract
- sqlike.shared    only SchemaChange and SchemaImage, which are not in the root shared/ module yet.
                   Every other shared type comes from shared/src/main/java (CCM keeps no copies).
- sqlike.ccm       CCM classes:
    ConcurrencyControlManager, CCMConfig, TransactionManager, Transaction   transactions and protocol choice (CC-01, CC-02)
    Resources                                                                helpers that build table, record, and catalog resources
    FRMBridge                                                                WAL logging, abort, recovery (CC-03, CC-04, CC-05)
    IConcurrencyProtocol, LockBasedProtocol                                  lock-based access (CC-06)
    DeadlockHandler                                                          deadlock handling (CC-07)
    TimestampProtocol                                                        timestamp-based access (CC-08)
    ScheduleOperation, ScheduleRecorder, SerializabilityVerifier             serializability check (CC-09)
    CCMDriver, Scenario, MockFRM                                             standalone driver (CC-10)

Build with Maven (Maven 3.9+), from the repository root:
  mvn -pl concurrency-control -am compile        compile CCM and the root shared module
  mvn -pl concurrency-control -am test-compile   also compile the tests in src/test/java
  mvn test-compile                               compile every component at once
Classes go to concurrency-control/target/classes and concurrency-control/target/test-classes.
The tests are standalone classes, not JUnit, so mvn test only compiles them. Run them with java:
  Linux / macOS:
    java -cp "concurrency-control/target/classes:concurrency-control/target/test-classes:shared/target/classes" sqlike.ccm.CCMDriver
  Windows (use ; instead of :):
    java -cp "concurrency-control/target/classes;concurrency-control/target/test-classes;shared/target/classes" sqlike.ccm.CCMDriver
Replace sqlike.ccm.CCMDriver with a test class such as sqlike.ccm.TransactionTest to run one test.

Build and run the driver without Maven (JDK 17 or newer), from the repository root:
  javac --release 17 -d out $(find shared/src/main/java concurrency-control/src/main/java -name '*.java')
  java -cp out sqlike.ccm.CCMDriver
The driver runs every scenario against ConcurrencyControlManager with MockFRM and prints
"Total: N/N skenario LULUS" when all of them pass.

Run all tests and the driver from the repository root:
  ./concurrency-control/run-tests.sh
The tests are standalone classes in src/test/java/sqlike/ccm (LockBasedProtocolTest,
TimestampProtocolTest, TransactionManagerTest, TransactionTest, CCMConfigTest, MockFRMTest,
FRMBridgeTest, ConcurrencyControlManagerTest). run-tests.sh runs every *Test class, then the driver.
FRM is a MockFRM here; integration with the real FRM and QP is Milestone 3.
